<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nymphaion</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Nymphaeum, Kemalpaşa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nymphaion was a town in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a>, east of Smyrna, now Kemalpaşa near İzmir in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>. The emperors of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaea">Nicaea</a> often resided there, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-iii-doukas-vatatzes">John III Doukas Vatatzes</a> built a palace whose ruins survive. In 1261 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-viii-palaiologos">Michael VIII Palaiologos</a> concluded the Treaty of Nymphaeum there with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a>, granting Genoese merchants wide privileges in return for naval support.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Nicaean emperor Michael VIII Palaiologos and the Republic of Genoa concluded a trade and defence pact at <b>Nymphaeum</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-13">13 March in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The Laskarid emperors governed through a Byzantine-style court and bureaucracy, with the patriarch residing at Nicaea and the court often at <b>Nymphaion</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/empire-of-nicaea">Empire of Nicaea</a></li></ul></section>
